  "answer_text": "Home pregnancy tests work by detecting a hormone called human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG), which your body begins producing after a fertilized egg implants in the uterus. Because reading these tests can sometimes be confusing or stressful, understanding the different types of results can help you determine your next steps. \n\n---\n\n### How to Interpret Your Results\n\n#### 1. Positive Result (Even a Faint One)\n* **What it looks like:** A clear second line, a plus sign, or the word \"Pregnant\" on a digital test.\n* **Faint lines:** If you see a second line that has **visible color** (pink or blue) and it appeared **within the test\u2019s recommended reading window** (usually 3 to 5 minutes), it is a positive result. In the very early stages of pregnancy, hCG levels are low, which makes the line faint. Because hCG levels typically double every 48 hours in early pregnancy, testing again in two days will usually yield a much darker, clearer line.\n* **Next steps:** If you have a positive test, schedule an appointment with an OB/GYN or midwife to confirm the pregnancy and discuss prenatal care. It is highly recommended to start taking a daily prenatal vitamin containing folic acid immediately, and to avoid alcohol, smoking, or any medications not cleared by a doctor.\n\n#### 2. Negative Result\n* **What it looks like:** Only the control line is visible, a minus sign, or the words \"Not Pregnant.\"\n* **Testing too early:** If you test before or right around the day of your missed period, your hCG levels might still be too low for the test to detect. \n* **Next steps:** If your period is late but your test is negative, wait 48 hours and test again using your first-morning urine, which is the most concentrated. If your period is more than a week late and you are still getting negative results, consult a healthcare provider to explore other reasons for a delayed cycle (such as stress, hormonal imbalances, or thyroid issues).\n\n#### 3. Evaporation Line (Commonly Confused for a Faint Positive)\n* **What it looks like:** A faint, colorless, grey, or shadow-like line where the second line should be.\n* **Why it happens:** This is not a positive result. It is a line left behind by the urine as it evaporates off the test strip. Evaporation lines typically appear **after** the manufacturer\u2019s recommended reading window has passed (usually 10 minutes or more after testing).\n* **Next steps:** If you suspect you looked at the test too late, discard it. Take a new test and make sure to read the result strictly within the 3-to-5-minute window.\n\n#### 4. Invalid Result\n* **What it looks like:** No lines appear at all, or a test line appears but the control line is missing.\n* **Next steps:** The test is faulty or did not absorb enough urine. Discard it and try again with a new test strip.\n\n---\n\n### Tips for Getting the Most Accurate Result\n* **Use first-morning urine:** Your urine is most concentrated when you first wake up, which makes it easier for the test to detect hCG.\n* **Time it carefully:** Set a timer on your phone. Do not read the test before the minimum time has passed, and do not look at it after 10 minutes.\n* **Try a digital test:** If interpreting faint lines is causing anxiety, a digital test that clearly displays \"Pregnant\" or \"Not Pregnant\" removes the guesswork.\n\nTo help me provide more tailored guidance, **could you share a few details about your situation?**\n1. What does your test result look like (e.g., are you seeing a very faint line, a digital reading, or no line)?\n2. How many days has it been since your expected or missed period?\n3. Did you read the test within the exact time window specified in the instructions?",
